{"text": " Because America did not buy those yen with dollars. It sold euros. And the European Central Bank only found out after the trades had cleared. Senior European officials told the Financial Times this broke decades of convention and that it had never happened before. The Treasury's answer, when they were asked, was that it doesn't coordinate with anyone on how it allocates that fund. Which is true, but is also basically the same as saying you plead the fifth. And there's one more thing in this timeline that's important. On July 23rd, the U.S. Treasury's Foreign Exchange Monitoring List was published. It's the official government watch list to track major U.S. trading partners and ensure they aren't artificially manipulating their currencies. On the list are countries like China, Germany, Korea, and most importantly Japan. Eight days later, the same building that released this report bought Japan's currency. So America broke a 28-year rule, paid by dumping Europe's currency, only told them after the trade was done and all for a country it had listed as a currency risk the week before. Why?", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.44, "text": " Because America did not buy those yen with dollars.", "tokens": [50363, 4362, 2253, 750, 407, 2822, 883, 28808, 351, 5054, 13, 50503], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12404654872032904, "compression_ratio": 1.63125, "no_speech_prob": 0.0032349599059671164, "words": [{"word": " Because", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.22, "probability": 0.5779945850372314}, {"word": " America", "start": 0.22, "end": 0.54, "probability": 0.9692251682281494}, {"word": " did", "start": 0.54, "end": 0.84, "probability": 0.989924430847168}, {"word": " not", "start": 0.84, "end": 1.06, "probability": 0.9980003237724304}, {"word": " buy", "start": 1.06, "end": 1.3, "probability": 0.9928874373435974}, {"word": " those", "start": 1.3, "end": 1.56, "probability": 0.9884217381477356}, {"word": " yen", "start": 1.56, "end": 1.8, "pro